If your EC2 instance restarts (either manually or unexpectedly), here's what you need to know:

𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗣𝗿𝗼𝗯𝗹𝗲𝗺
- When an EC2 instance is restarted or stopped and started again, the public IP changes (unless it's an Elastic IP).
- If your DNS is mapped to the old public IP, users will no longer be able to access your application.
- This leads to downtime even though your application is still running.

𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗥𝗼𝗼𝘁 𝗖𝗮𝘂𝘀𝗲

Your DNS is pointing to a dynamic public IP. After a restart, that IP is no longer valid. The new IP needs to be updated in your DNS for the traffic to reach your application again.

𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗦𝗼𝗹𝘂𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻
Use an Elastic IP a static IP provided by AWS that stays the same even after restarts.
Steps to fix it:
- Go to the EC2 Dashboard -> Elastic IPs
- Allocate a new Elastic IP
- Associate it with your instance
- Update your DNS to this new static IP
Now, even if your EC2 restarts, your app remains reachable.

𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗜𝗻𝘀𝗶𝗴𝗵𝘁
If you're running production workloads or auto-scaling environments, consider:
- Using a 𝗟𝗼𝗮𝗱 𝗕𝗮𝗹𝗮𝗻𝗰𝗲𝗿 instead of exposing EC2 directly
- Managing DNS with 𝗥𝗼𝘂𝘁𝗲 𝟱𝟯 + 𝗛𝗲𝗮𝗹𝘁𝗵 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸𝘀 for high availability

Avoiding downtime is all about planning. Something as simple as IP management can save hours of debugging later.
